<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sourou Migan Apithy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sourou Migan Apithy was a Dahomeyan politician whose base was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-novo">Porto-Novo</a> and the south-east. Elected to the French Constituent Assemblies in 1945–1946 and then the National Assembly, he was the first prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> in 1958–1959, vice-president under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubert-maga">Hubert Maga</a> and president from 1964 to 1965. He later sat on the Presidential Council of 1970–1972.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sourou-migan-apithy.jpg" alt="Sourou Migan Apithy" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown, Présence Congolaise · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sourou_Migan_Apithy_in_1958.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sourou-Migan Apithy was born on 8 April 1913 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-novo">Porto-Novo</a>. He descended from an Ogu royal family but was not born into privilege, and his middle name, Migan, signified family ties with the chief ministers of the old Dahomeyan kingdoms. He studied at mission schools, became an assistant teacher, and in 1933 went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he qualified in accounting. In 1939 and 1940 he fought as an artillery officer, having volunteered for the army. Not until 1945 did he return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>, when the Catholic pastor Francis Aupiais encouraged him to enter politics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Running as a Socialist, Apithy was elected in 1945 to represent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> at the French Constituent Assembly, with 6,600 of 9,057 votes. His nomination was a strategic move by Europeans, who wished to elect a black person to appease the colonists while keeping full power. In November 1946 he won Dahomey's single seat in the French National Assembly. There he sat on commissions on overseas territories and finance, and proposed a bank covering French West Africa and Togo, which became law.</p><p>The election of 17 June 1951 brought a second seat and a new rival, the northern teacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubert-maga">Hubert Maga</a>. Apithy won 53,463 of 147,350 votes, and 64 per cent of them came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-novo">Porto-Novo</a> area. This election is cited as the point when regionalist parties arose, and it produced three de facto zones led by Maga in the north, Apithy in the south-east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ahomadegbe-tometin">Justin Ahomadégbé-Tomêtin</a> in the south-west. Apithy's support came mostly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, Maga's from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bariba">Bariba</a> and Ahomadégbé-Tomêtin's from the Fon and Goun. He became mayor of Porto-Novo in 1956 and later president of the Territorial Assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>.</p><p>He was the second president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> from 25 January 1964 until 27 November 1965, when Christophe Soglo overthrew him after infighting in the government, and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. After a disputed election on 28 March 1970, the three leaders formed a presidential council on 7 May, with the chair rotating every two years. Apithy shared in the council's decisions, among them the expulsion of the Togolese opposition leader Noe Kutuklui in 1971, which sparked demonstrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotonou">Cotonou</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 26 October 1972 soldiers led by Major Mathieu Kérékou seized power, calling the triumvirate &quot;truly a monster&quot;. Maga, Ahomadégbé-Tomêtin and Apithy spent more than nine years in prison before Kérékou freed them in 1981. Apithy then settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he died on 3 December 1989, a few days before Kérékou ended socialism in the country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sourou-Migan Apithy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1960 coups and regime changes followed, dominated by Hubert Maga, <b>Sourou Migan Apithy</b>, Justin Ahomadégbé and Émile Derlin Zinsou.</blockquote><a href="/state/benin">Benin</a></li><li><blockquote>Politics after 1946 was divided along regional lines, led by <b>Sourou Migan Apithy</b>, Hubert Maga and Justin Ahomadegbe.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-dahomey">French Dahomey</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-novo">Porto-Novo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/benin">Benin</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.48,2.62&amp;year=1962" aria-label="Porto-Novo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4622 1921 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sourou Migan Apithy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5072 2191h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5072 2191h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=6.48,2.62&amp;year=1962">What was here in 1962? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
